  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry, tranquil and increasingly milder weather \nextends right through Saturday or even Sunday.\n\n8H ridge axis is practically visibly with the change of\nadvection marked by what was a very sharp back/western edge to \nthe stratus. Some muddying of the waters is occurring, but the\noverwhelming message is that our cloud deck is not long for this\nworld. Expect fog to fill in some of the valleys, esp in the\nwake of those lower clouds. Dewpoints are headed downward in the\nSW. The fog may form in places that are/will be freezing this \nmorning. We'll consider but probably not issue a freezing fog \nadvisory. Collaboration will be key on that matter.\n\nA string of sunny and increasingly warmer days followed by\nmainly clear and chilly nights is ahead for the remainder of \nthe week into the weekend. Fcst Max temps peak 10-20 degrees \nabove the historical average Thu-Sun while nights will run \ncloser to climo resulting in large diurnal ranges indicative of \na prolonged late Autumn dry spell. Expect ideal conditions for \noutdoor pre-winter activities: raking leaves, set up holiday \ndecorations, or enjoying time outside, it will be an very \npleasant stretch of weather for the 3rd week of November. \n\nNBM Dewpoints through Thursday may be too high given the\nexpected sunshine and light wind. GFS MOS paints a drier outcome\nand is supported by the forecast soundings being very dry above\nthe sfc/blyr. Mixing to 2500ft is expected today and Wed, and it\nshould rise higher on Thursday. So, Thursday will probably tap\nthe driest air and be the least humid day of the bunch, esp\nconsidering the expected daily rise in temps.\n\nWith the resumption of another prolonged dry stretch, fire \nweather concerns could increase as we get later into November \nand December. Any particularly windy patterns with minimal \nprecipitation could lead to an elevated risk of rapid wildfire \nspread.",
